<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.Verify.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Versions come from the repo-root Directory.Packages.props (F-38, central package management). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    No device and no socket. Every test drives a SCRIPTED transport and a RECORDING connect factory, so
    "the fence refused and nothing connected" is asserted against a counter rather than against an exit
    code: a disconnected gate can still produce the right exit code by accident.

    The fence tests use the REAL DeviceAccessGuard against a REAL temporary allowlist file, not an
    interface over it. A seam there would let the tests exercise a fence that is not the one an operator
    runs.

    NOTE: this project deliberately CONTAINS a class that calls WriteHoldingRegisters
    (PlantedWriteControl). It is the live positive control for the IL walk, and it must stay here and
    never move into the shipped assembly.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Verify\Harness.Verify.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
